  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nCentral IL is currently positioned between high pressure centered \nover eastern VA and low pressure over southeast MT. The pressure \ngradient between these systems will increase slightly this afternoon \nas the low tracks through ND, bringing breezy 10-15 mph south winds \nwith gusts 15-20 mph for most of central IL, with the breeziest \nconditions north of I-72. Temperatures will also continue the \nwarming trend, with highs in the lower 60s. Lows tonight look to \nremain above 40 for most areas north of I-70 with a light southerly \nbreeze limiting the nocturnal near-surface radiational temperature \ninversion. The low will pass by well to the north in Canada with the \npressure gradient relaxing considerably Thursday. Weak southerly \nflow will keep the warming trend going, with highs reaching the mid \nto upper 60s.\n\nThe next system looks to begin impacting central IL Friday evening \nwith increased southerly breezes, however with a low track having \nalso trended farther north, similar to the Wed/Thur system, \nprecipitation looks to hold off until Saturday evening as the \ntrailing cold front finally approaches central IL, driven by an \nupper level pattern shift with a more progressive flow patten across \nthe central U.S and a shortwave embedded in this flow. While there \nis still uncertainty in timing this far out, probability of \nprecipitation (PoP) ranges from 20 percent in southeast IL to 60 \npercent NW of the IL River Saturday night, with 40-50 percent PoP \ncontinuing Sunday. The front looks to traverse central IL during the \nday Sunday, causing temperatures to drop off from Friday and \nSaturday's upper 60s highs, to 50s for Sunday. Highs in the 40s \nshould prevail Monday through Wednesday following Sunday's cold \nfront, with dry conditions \n\nWill also need to keep an eye on the potential for a few \nthunderstorms Saturday night or Sunday as a corridor of dewpoints in \nthe mid to upper 50s looks to develop ahead of the front, and could \npotentially result in a combination of low CAPE/moderate shear. 12Z \nensembles indicate 10-20 percent joint probability of 100 J/kg \nMUCAPE with 30 kts deep layer shear.",
